# RFP Hub client — local environment
#
# Copy this to `packages/frontend/.env.local`, which Next reads automatically and which is
# gitignored:
#
#   cp .env-example .env.local
#   pnpm --filter @the-rfp-hub/frontend dev
#
# ONE REQUIRED VARIABLE AND TWO OPTIONAL ONES. There used to be a different two: the API's origin,
# and a third-party auth application id that had to be a DIFFERENT application per environment or a
# test login in development became a real account in production's directory. Sessions are now issued
# by the API itself, so that whole class of misconfiguration is gone. The two commented slots below
# are newer and unrelated: an analytics id, and the one origin THIS deployment is indexable at. Both
# are left unset here on purpose.
#
# IT IS `NEXT_PUBLIC_`, WHICH MEANS TWO THINGS.
#
#   1. It is INLINED INTO THE BUNDLE AT BUILD TIME. Changing it on a running host does nothing;
#      the deployment has to be rebuilt. The frontend says so on screen when NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL is
#      missing, because this is the single most common way to lose an afternoon here.
#   2. It is PUBLIC. Anyone who loads the page can read it. Neither is a secret — an origin is an
#      identifier — and NOTHING SECRET MAY EVER BE ADDED WITH THIS PREFIX. This package holds no
#      server-side credential at all: every authenticated request is made by the browser with the
#      signed-in user's own session token.

# Where the API lives. No trailing slash needed (one is trimmed). REQUIRED in every environment.
#
# This origin does three jobs: it is what `/v1` is called on, it is where Better-Auth is mounted
# (`/api/auth`), and it is written into the page's Content-Security-Policy `connect-src` — so the
# browser may talk to this API and to nothing else. That is why a deployment pointing at a
# different API must be rebuilt rather than reconfigured.
#
# The API must list this frontend's own origin in its TRUSTED_ORIGINS, or the browser's preflight
# for the sign-in calls is refused.
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3001

# OPTIONAL: a Google Analytics 4 measurement id (G-…). Leave unset — the default, and what a fork
# should keep — and no analytics loads and the CSP names no Google origin. Set it and the layout
# loads gtag.js, with the CSP opened to exactly the origins GA4 needs (see src/lib/csp.ts).
# Enabling it on a deployment has privacy-page consequences: src/app/privacy/page.tsx must keep
# describing what that deployment actually does.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GA_ID=

# The ONE origin this deployment considers itself the canonical, indexable copy of the site.
# OPTIONAL, and left UNSET here on purpose.
#
# ON VERCEL THERE IS NOTHING TO CONFIGURE: production is detected from Vercel's own VERCEL_ENV and
# VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL, and a preview — being anything but `production` — stays noindex.
# ANYWHERE ELSE, set this to opt that deployment into indexing; it also wins over the Vercel-derived
# value where both exist. Local dev, staging and every preview must leave it unset —
# src/app/layout.tsx, sitemap.ts and robots.ts compare the canonical origin against the incoming
# request's own and only index / publish a sitemap / allow crawling when the two match, so this
# being unset is what keeps this dev server (and staging, and a preview) out of search results.
# See packages/frontend/README.md's "Environment" and "Deployment" sections.
#
# It must be the SCHEME AND HOST A BROWSER ACTUALLY USES, exactly: `http://` where visitors get
# `https://`, or an internal hostname a proxy rewrites `Host` to, never matches and the deployment
# stays noindex forever. `X-Forwarded-Host` is read before `Host` for that reason. A `www.` alias
# belongs behind a redirect to this origin, not on a second deployment of its own.
#
# THE APP TRUSTS `X-Forwarded-Host`: run it behind a proxy that sets or overwrites that header
# (Vercel does), or do not expose it directly. A requester who forges it only changes their own
# response, never what a crawler is told, but the header is meant to be the edge's statement.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_ORIGIN=
